From 737360e1ab7799203b2fa2ab068abc5a4fe1a7f2 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Thu, 13 Aug 2026 17:39:20 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 09080/11362] Enforce recording conventions adversarially: the
 fifth audit reviews ARTIFACTS, not code

REQ-OPS-ARTIFACT-CONVENTION-6264. Operator: "it sounds like we need to enforce
conventions with our adversarial agent." Correct, and placement matters, so this
ships in two halves.

PREVENTION, planner-side, no LLM. Two conventions added to the REQUIRED ARTIFACT
FIELDS guidance beside solve_provenance: per_unit_rows for any comparative claim
(57 of the 60 most recent artifacts carry none), and blocked_reason for any
blocked_* verdict (37 of 58 record nothing, and all 31 carrying
blocked_gate_check_failed say nothing about which gate failed). A convention in
the task spec changes what gets WRITTEN; an audit can only notice afterwards that
it was not.

DETECTION, adversarial, milestone close. artifact_convention_audit.py is the FIFTH
audit, beside four that already run -- and all four of those review CODE or DOCS.
None reviewed the ARTIFACTS, which are the research record every downstream claim
rests on. It asks one question: could a stranger CHECK this claim, or must they
trust it? Reuses the siblings' audit-integrity guard so a verdict quoting absent
evidence is downgraded rather than acted on. Never edits, never blocks, bounded
with --recent 8.

Adversarial rather than another lint on the evidence, not on preference:
verdict_row_consistency_lint tried pattern matching on this same problem and
needed five rounds of widening, each a real gap, and still misses cases and emits
noise. The pattern-narrower-than-its-concept failure recurred five times inside
the tool built to catch it. "Is this claim checkable" is semantic; a reviewer
needs the question, not every artifact shape.

First live run earned its place. It flagged experiment_3392 AGGREGATE_ONLY: the
artifact claims the archive is complete and activation ready, backed by counts,
while recording nothing about WHICH upstream artifacts were checked or what each
produced. Its missing-check statement is exactly right and no pattern list would
have produced it. Quoted fields are genuinely present so the integrity guard
passed it as real. It marked two others CHECKABLE, so it is not flagging
everything.

One bug recorded rather than quietly fixed: the first wiring gated on
AUDIT_ENABLED, a name I invented that exists nowhere in the file. It compiled
because the guard is only evaluated at runtime, and would have raised NameError at
the first milestone close, taking the retrospective with it. Now gated on
`not dry_run`, the same condition as its siblings.

